AB  - In this work, a nonlocal diffusion viral infection within-host model with logistic hepatocyte growth is analyzed. The latter takes into account absorption effect and the usage of the standard incidence force for viral infection in a spatially heterogeneous environment. We first establish the well-posedness, boundedness and asymptotic smoothness of this model. Furthermore, the perturbation technique has enabled us to define the basic reproduction number & Rscr;(0) and a threshold tau(0) such that if & Rscr;(0) <= tau(0) < 1, the infection-free equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable. Next, we show that the model is uniformly persistent when & Rscr;(0)> 1. Finally, we present some numerical simulations to confirm our obtained theoretical results.
